1
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,944
IDA: Evening, old street.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,106
Every evening, for more than a century,

3
00:00:18,146 --> 00:00:23,284
someone in a city like this walked out
at dusk with a ladder and a long pole,

4
00:00:23,324 --> 00:00:25,478
and gave the street its night.

5
00:00:25,832 --> 00:00:29,368
Ida isn't real, and her
street has no name.

6
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:31,887
But her job was real.

7
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:36,322
This channel is about jobs
like hers: the work people do,

8
00:00:36,362 --> 00:00:39,549
and what happens to it when
machines learn to do it.

9
00:00:40,112 --> 00:00:43,090
Ida lights a lamp for each of those jobs.

10
00:00:43,165 --> 00:00:46,597
Tonight, she starts with her own.

11
00:00:51,333 --> 00:00:53,464
London, 1807.

12
00:00:53,876 --> 00:00:59,153
On one street, Pall Mall, a row of
lamps burning coal gas was lit.

13
00:00:59,321 --> 00:01:02,914
It is remembered as the first
street in the world lit that way.

14
00:01:03,152 --> 00:01:05,248
The idea spread fast.

15
00:01:05,311 --> 00:01:10,426
Within a few years, gas lamps ran along
street after street across London.

16
00:01:10,687 --> 00:01:17,543
And every one of those lamps needed
a person, every dusk and every dawn.

17
00:01:19,975 --> 00:01:25,322
That person's shift was called a round:
dozens of lamps along a fixed route,

18
00:01:25,362 --> 00:01:27,795
lit at dusk and put out at dawn.

19
00:01:28,090 --> 00:01:32,230
In between, you cleaned the glass
and reported the broken ones.

20
00:01:32,618 --> 00:01:36,444
Rain, fog or frost, the
lamps had to be lit.

21
00:01:36,704 --> 00:01:41,250
It was, almost entirely,
one task, repeated.

22
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:42,794
Remember that.

23
00:01:42,927 --> 00:01:45,581
It matters later.

24
00:01:46,917 --> 00:01:50,092
IDA: Not yet… not yet.

25
00:01:50,917 --> 00:01:53,000
Ida's watch runs seven minutes slow.

26
00:01:54,354 --> 00:01:55,590
She knows it.

27
00:01:55,944 --> 00:01:58,922
The city's clock is moving on.

28
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:04,526
Then came a different kind of light.

29
00:02:04,856 --> 00:02:11,596
In 1878, Paris strung sixty-four electric
arc lamps along and around the Avenue de

30
00:02:11,636 --> 00:02:12,636
l'Opéra.

31
00:02:12,786 --> 00:02:15,033
They needed nobody on a ladder.

32
00:02:15,212 --> 00:02:20,594
It was a slow tide, not a single night:
a white lamp on the main avenue,

33
00:02:20,669 --> 00:02:25,876
a few more on the side streets, and
every round got a little shorter.

34
00:02:26,808 --> 00:02:32,422
In April 1907, New York's lamplighters
went on strike for union recognition and

35
00:02:32,462 --> 00:02:33,594
better pay.

36
00:02:33,948 --> 00:02:35,858
Parts of the city went dark.

37
00:02:36,119 --> 00:02:39,156
Police officers were sent
out to light the lamps,

38
00:02:39,196 --> 00:02:41,733
some climbing the posts with matches.

39
00:02:41,993 --> 00:02:44,891
Within about a week, the
men were back at work.

40
00:02:45,337 --> 00:02:48,884
A strike only works if the
city can't do without you,

41
00:02:49,076 --> 00:02:53,296
and every year the city could
do a little more without them.

42
00:02:54,267 --> 00:02:56,549
Here is what most people don't know.

43
00:02:56,891 --> 00:03:01,971
London still has gas street lamps,
in Covent Garden, along the Mall,

44
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:04,502
outside palaces and theatres.

45
00:03:04,855 --> 00:03:10,365
British Gas says its team looked after
about eleven hundred of them in 2023.

46
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,489
That team is five people.

47
00:03:12,564 --> 00:03:14,103
Five lamplighters.

48
00:03:14,143 --> 00:03:16,239
They don't carry fire anymore.

49
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:19,537
Most lamps run on small clockwork timers,

50
00:03:19,577 --> 00:03:24,889
and every two weeks someone climbs up
to wind the clock and check the lamp.

51
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:31,535
Lamplighting was one job built
almost entirely around one task,

52
00:03:31,575 --> 00:03:36,133
and that is exactly the kind of
job a machine can take whole.

53
00:03:36,393 --> 00:03:38,710
The next one came indoors.

54
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:39,163
U.S.

55
00:03:39,203 --> 00:03:44,643
census counts show about thirteen
thousand elevator operators in 1900,

56
00:03:44,683 --> 00:03:47,998
and nearly a hundred thousand by 1950.

57
00:03:48,189 --> 00:03:51,121
Then elevators began to run themselves.

58
00:03:51,184 --> 00:03:54,813
By 1970, thirty-seven thousand were left.

59
00:03:54,992 --> 00:04:00,141
The economist James Bessen followed about
two hundred and seventy occupations from

60
00:04:00,181 --> 00:04:03,334
the 1950 census to 2010.

61
00:04:03,781 --> 00:04:05,900
Many shrank or changed.

62
00:04:06,091 --> 00:04:11,821
Only one disappeared mainly because
of automation: the elevator operator.

63
00:04:14,267 --> 00:04:17,141
The same census tables
tell a second story.

64
00:04:17,576 --> 00:04:22,400
Telephone operators: about
nineteen thousand in 1900,

65
00:04:22,731 --> 00:04:26,533
about four hundred and
twenty thousand by 1970.

66
00:04:26,957 --> 00:04:31,108
Stenographers, typists and
secretaries, counted together:

67
00:04:31,531 --> 00:04:35,612
from about a hundred and thirty
thousand to nearly four million.

68
00:04:35,966 --> 00:04:40,988
In 1970, those were the
jobs of the modern office.

69
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:45,369
Ida's lamp still burns at
the end of the street.

70
00:04:45,816 --> 00:04:48,202
Behind it, the office lights are on.

71
00:04:48,393 --> 00:04:53,078
In the 1960s, the job on its way
out was the elevator operator.

72
00:04:53,339 --> 00:04:54,739
Today, the U.S.

73
00:04:54,779 --> 00:04:58,047
Bureau of Labor Statistics
has a list of its own:

74
00:04:58,308 --> 00:05:04,665
the jobs it projects will shrink
fastest between 2025 and 2035.

75
00:05:04,833 --> 00:05:09,785
At the top, word processors and
typists: down thirty-four percent.

76
00:05:09,849 --> 00:05:12,525
Telephone operators, down twenty-eight.

77
00:05:12,635 --> 00:05:17,274
Switchboard operators and data
entry keyers, down about a quarter.

78
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:19,700
Telemarketers, down a fifth.

79
00:05:20,019 --> 00:05:21,628
Not the same technology.

80
00:05:21,668 --> 00:05:26,817
The same pattern: one task, repeated,
and a machine that can repeat it.

81
00:05:26,985 --> 00:05:29,929
The Bureau names AI in its forecasts.

82
00:05:30,085 --> 00:05:33,110
In its 2023 to 2033 projections,

83
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:38,625
it expected AI to mostly affect jobs whose
core tasks generative AI can most easily

84
00:05:38,665 --> 00:05:39,665
copy.

85
00:05:39,953 --> 00:05:43,675
Customer service representatives:
down five percent.

86
00:05:44,110 --> 00:05:46,867
Auto damage appraisers: down nine.

87
00:05:46,989 --> 00:05:49,863
Claims adjusters: down about four.

88
00:05:50,205 --> 00:05:51,709
Some jobs grow.

89
00:05:51,958 --> 00:05:56,817
In that same round, software
developers: up almost eighteen percent.

90
00:05:57,170 --> 00:06:03,702
In the newest projections, for 2025 to
2035: nurse practitioners up forty-one,

91
00:06:03,742 --> 00:06:09,402
solar installers up more than thirty-six,
data scientists up about thirty-five.

92
00:06:09,663 --> 00:06:10,970
Some jobs end.

93
00:06:11,010 --> 00:06:12,212
Some change.

94
00:06:12,287 --> 00:06:13,558
Some are new.

95
00:06:15,683 --> 00:06:21,076
To compare speeds fairly, put them on
the same clock: change over ten years.

96
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:26,719
Elevator operators, counted by the
census: down almost a fifth in the 1950s,

97
00:06:26,759 --> 00:06:29,284
then by half in the 1960s.

98
00:06:29,522 --> 00:06:33,441
Word processors and typists,
projected by the Bureau:

99
00:06:33,574 --> 00:06:36,564
down about a third over
the next ten years.

100
00:06:36,825 --> 00:06:39,850
One is history, the other a forecast.

101
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:44,261
The census counted people;
the Bureau counts jobs.

102
00:06:44,522 --> 00:06:47,187
But the size is of the same order.

103
00:06:47,529 --> 00:06:49,509
So what is different this time?

104
00:06:49,863 --> 00:06:54,432
The elevator was one machine replacing
one job in one kind of building.

105
00:06:54,739 --> 00:07:00,620
AI reaches into many office and knowledge
jobs at once: writing, answering,

106
00:07:00,672 --> 00:07:02,466
checking, coding.

107
00:07:02,866 --> 00:07:04,440
The pattern is old.

108
00:07:04,654 --> 00:07:06,251
The reach is new.

109
00:07:06,558 --> 00:07:08,979
One forecast, one technology,

110
00:07:09,019 --> 00:07:14,587
and its case studies run from call centres
to insurance offices to law firms.

111
00:07:18,517 --> 00:07:20,474
The Bureau says why.

112
00:07:20,514 --> 00:07:23,167
Across office and administrative support,

113
00:07:23,207 --> 00:07:28,276
it projects employment to fall about
four percent, as automation tools,

114
00:07:28,316 --> 00:07:32,281
including those powered by
AI, take on more of the work.

115
00:07:32,542 --> 00:07:33,988
Meanwhile total U.S.

116
00:07:34,028 --> 00:07:37,854
employment is projected to grow
about three and a half percent.

117
00:07:40,308 --> 00:07:46,305
Remember Bessen's two hundred and seventy
jobs: machines mostly took over tasks,

118
00:07:46,345 --> 00:07:47,698
not whole jobs.

119
00:07:47,738 --> 00:07:50,694
Many jobs that AI touches
are still growing.

120
00:07:50,827 --> 00:07:52,052
Take lawyers.

121
00:07:52,092 --> 00:07:59,262
In that 2023 to 2033 round, the Bureau
expected AI to speed up parts of legal work,

122
00:07:59,337 --> 00:08:03,279
but said its output still needs
detailed review by lawyers.

123
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:06,704
Lawyers were projected to
grow about five percent,

124
00:08:06,779 --> 00:08:10,152
close to the four percent
average for all jobs.

125
00:08:10,506 --> 00:08:13,171
Paralegals, about one percent.

126
00:08:13,524 --> 00:08:18,546
In finance, the same round had credit
analysts down about four percent,

127
00:08:18,621 --> 00:08:21,472
but financial analysts up almost ten.

128
00:08:21,663 --> 00:08:23,237
The work changes.

129
00:08:23,277 --> 00:08:25,373
The job remains.

130
00:08:27,808 --> 00:08:29,799
One early warning sign.

131
00:08:30,246 --> 00:08:35,175
Researchers at Stanford's Digital Economy
Lab looked at payroll data through June

132
00:08:35,215 --> 00:08:36,707
2026.

133
00:08:37,050 --> 00:08:42,919
Workers aged twenty-two to twenty-five in
the jobs most exposed to AI were employed

134
00:08:42,959 --> 00:08:46,854
at about nineteen percent below
where they would otherwise be,

135
00:08:46,929 --> 00:08:49,339
mostly because fewer were hired.

136
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:52,880
More experienced workers
showed no such gap.

137
00:08:53,071 --> 00:08:58,522
The researchers call it an early
signal, not proof that AI caused it.

138
00:09:01,475 --> 00:09:03,548
Forecasts are not verdicts.

139
00:09:03,681 --> 00:09:06,659
Some of these jobs will
end, many will change,

140
00:09:06,699 --> 00:09:10,119
and new ones will appear that
nobody has a name for yet.

141
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:15,088
These numbers describe a whole
country, not any one person's future.

142
00:09:15,442 --> 00:09:17,515
But they tell us where to look.

143
00:09:17,869 --> 00:09:20,650
The lamplighters had no list like this.

144
00:09:21,003 --> 00:09:22,670
We do.

145
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:27,275
Ida lights one lamp for every
job that ends this way.

146
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:31,037
Tonight, her second lamp is
for the elevator operators.

147
00:09:31,379 --> 00:09:33,173
Cas holds the ladder.

148
00:09:33,527 --> 00:09:38,014
He will grow up in the white city,
but he knows what a lamp is for.

149
00:09:45,764 --> 00:09:48,800
Every era has its last lamplighters.

150
00:09:49,154 --> 00:09:52,376
So here is the question this
channel will keep asking:

151
00:09:52,822 --> 00:09:55,824
Who are the last lamplighters of our time?
